00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.800
in the comment section below please.
Today, we're going to look at - I have I

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:25.350
hold a piece of paper here - four questions
in four different languages and we're

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.000
going to start with English.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:59.850
Thank you very much happier on a horse I don't know your real name, just your YouTube name and I'll

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:06.960
get to you in a second, let me put this down. So, imagine a prism and imagine this prism

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:14.549
- to be differentiated from prison -  so a
prism that is hit by light - normally

00:01:14.549 --> 00:01:19.890
that's the way we can dissect light and
we can see the multiple components of

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:27.210
light. Human beings are like that prism
and languages are those different colors

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:31.560
of light that we can see I don't think
that speaking languages changes my

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:37.320
personality per se because I always have
my own personality but it shows

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.180
different facets of my personality so I
think that the more languages you speak

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.759
the more facets of your personality you
can actually show to the rest of the

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:56.430
world and you can discover yourself and
of course I do change my cospeech gestures my

00:01:56.430 --> 00:02:01.320
hand gestures, my facial features, the
movement of my body, the way I'm speaking

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.930
now the very same gestures I'm using now
are not the gestures I would use if I

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:12.090
were speaking Italian but that's just an
external code of communicating I'm just

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:16.290
using a different channel,
so remember: my personality does not

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:25.050
change but what changes is the colorful
possibilities that I have to show my

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:30.600
soul to the rest of the world now on to
the second question asked by Raul

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:36.330
Carvajal in French so I will reply in
French. Hi Luca, which was your first motivation

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:44.330
to learn other languages. Are there languages you have started and given up on

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:51.510
because you lost interest?

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.110
The first languages that I learned at school  - didn't have much choice there - were English

00:02:55.110 --> 00:03:00.690
and French, they were the only  languages available

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:06.420
so I didn't make any deliberate decision, I just had to learn them.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:12.620
After that, I started learning German because I had seen some ad on TV about a course

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:17.610
and a friend of my parents spoke German really well, and I had heard that German was a really interesting language,

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:23.040
that German philosophy and literature are very rich, and that's how it all started.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:32.700
I started learning Spanish with the same exact course I had used to start learning German

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:36.780
and it had worked so well that I gave it a shot with Spanish as well.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:44.750
After that, I met a Dutch girl in Sardinia, and I started learning Dutch,

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:51.690
and there are other specific reasons for each language I have learned.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:57.080
It varied according to various circumstances

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:06.180
As for the second question, I started learning Romanian around 6 years ago (actually 9 years ago*),

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:13.920
and I gave up on it, and I gave up on it because..well we are here outdoors, well..

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:20.060
so if you hear dogs, cats and birds, that's normal.. started learning Romanian

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:26.940
because I told myself that I wanted to learn a relatively easy language,

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:30.510
together with a more difficult language - which was Japanese -

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:37.410
and I ended up giving up on both, and I gave up on Romanian because..well, Romanian is a relatively easy language,

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:43.140
but I think that the really problem was that I didn't find native speakers.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:48.000
or maybe I didn't want to find native speakers, people to talk to,

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.330
so I didn't establish relationships with people, I didn't start using the language

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:59.760
and I didn't get passionate about it, I think that's the real reason why I gave up on Romanian,

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.810
I didn't feel the passion - I really need to use a language somehow when I start using it.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:11.570
As far as Japanese is concerned, I made a video about it, where I talked about the fact that I started learning

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:18.990
through a method that didn't work that well for this specific language, so it has slowed me down a lot

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:25.320
and it has discouraged me, especially because Japanese is a rather complicated language. The third question

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:30.270
from Chile that's his name from Chile
and he has asked me this question in Spanish

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:35.130
Luca, I have a big doubt: you mentioned that at the very beginning, during the first 6 months,

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:41.160
what you do is to translate texts in order to learn a language, but my doubt is

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:46.680
how can you translate without knowing much about grammar or vocabulary

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:52.020
because the task is not to translate a paragraph by throwing the text into Google Translate,

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:56.580
or translating it by looking up every single word, how do you do that

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:00.270
without having any foundation in your target language? It is a necessary question

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:03.930
for me and for many others, thanks a lot Luca

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:15.270
Greetings from Chile. Ok. In order to reply to your question, I would say

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:20.280
that you can't start from scratch by using texts you randomly find on the internet,

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:25.290
You have to find texts which are COMPREHENSIBLE and which are

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:29.820
possibly bilingual and which come with audio. This is the reason why I always

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:36.539
this bidirectional Translation Technique to ASSIMIL, which is a series

00:06:36.539 --> 00:06:41.610
for those who want to learn foreign languages, because it provides a bilingual text

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:47.840
and this format gives me the possibility of using my mother tongue and my target language

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:55.229
in order to translate back and forth. So, I don't need to translate..I don't need to look up vocabulary

00:06:55.229 --> 00:07:01.590
or find grammar explanations because everything is already well explained there. If I want to, I can simply compare

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:11.879
the structure of the 2 languages, and on top of that, if there are things or points that I do not understand by simply comparing the two languages, I can always take a look at the grammar

00:07:11.879 --> 00:07:16.590
explanation that are there. So my piece of advice is

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:21.690
in order to implement the Bidirectional Translation you need to get hold of material

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:28.050
which is both comprehensible and enjoyable, and this is the most important point, and

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:32.819
thanks to the internet, you don't necessarily have to use ASSIMIL, you could use podcasts,

00:07:32.819 --> 00:07:39.180
other material and resources that you can find on the internet,

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:44.069
provided, once again, that this material be comprehensible

00:07:44.069 --> 00:07:50.250
that is with audio, with text - a transcription of the audio itself and you could use

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:56.819
Google Translate that in many cases allows one

00:07:56.819 --> 00:08:02.669
keep resorting to an efficient bidirectional translation.

00:08:02.669 --> 00:08:08.759
And now on to the fourth
and final question that Marvelenia asked.

00:08:08.759 --> 00:08:13.830
Hi Luca I have figured out that it is very important

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:17.759
to take care of how one eats,

00:08:17.759 --> 00:08:23.430
because eating well allows you to keep a clear head and engage longer in one's activities.

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:29.610
Also drinking enough (water) is important. A vegan diet with lots of raw food and little sugar

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:33.060
has turned out to be the best option for me. I would be interested in

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:38.880
hearing your opinion, thanks and lovely greetings.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:45.240
Thanks for the question Marvelenia. It is somewhat funny -

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:50.579
I have been making an effort to address questions (about language learning)

00:08:50.579 --> 00:08:55.410
since a lot of people ask me all the time "how do you learn a foreign language"

00:08:55.410 --> 00:09:08.730
but recently I have realized and understood that in order to learn a language well, you have to take care of yourself (first and foremost), and in this regard, 3 things are

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:17.040
really relevant, and they are: eating well, sleeping well, and avoid distractions.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:27.240
Since I started to wake up early, to make my bed,

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:36.000
I have started to avoid turning on my phone and staring at it

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:44.339
since I have started to replace it with reading, I have started eating a fruit at breakfast,

00:09:44.339 --> 00:09:53.009
eating a lot of beans and vegetables during the day, go to bed early, everything has started changing

00:09:53.009 --> 00:09:58.370
I have more willpower, more energy, I focus better, I wake up and I feel like learning

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:06.959
learning languages, going for a run in the park, working

00:10:06.959 --> 00:10:12.180
and that's why taking care of yourself is quite important. Recently I have read a very interesting book

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:19.800
that talks about how the Internet is changing the way our mind works, and I think that we all have

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:24.649
a big problem when it comes to focusing, and I think that

00:10:24.649 --> 00:10:30.509
in order to learn languages, you should work on yourself

00:10:30.509 --> 00:10:35.399
and on your concentration. So there you have it I've entered these four questions in

00:10:35.399 --> 00:10:39.540
four different languages if you want to
know more please ask in the comment

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:43.110
section below. Thank you for all the very
interesting questions that you've been

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:48.059
asking so far and I'm eager to reply to
more in more languages.

00:10:48.059 --> 00:10:52.050
Thank you very much for listening to me
and see you very soon here on YouTube

